questions.answer:
const runSequential = async (tasks) => {
  const results = [];
  for (const task of tasks) {
    results.push(await task());
  }
  return results;
};

const wait = (ms, value) =>
  new Promise((resolve) => setTimeout(() => resolve(value), ms));

runSequential([
  () => wait(20, "Kakade"),
  () => wait(5, "Asha"),
]).then(console.log);
// [ 'Kakade', 'Asha' ]

await inside the loop pauses before starting the next task, which is the sequential guarantee. Starting them all first and awaiting later (for example with map) would run them in parallel. for...of is the right loop because forEach cannot await. An empty tasks array returns [] immediately. Use Promise.all instead when the tasks are independent and can run concurrently.

