questions.answer:
const summarize = async (promises) => {
  const results = await Promise.allSettled(promises);
  const summary = { fulfilled: 0, rejected: 0, errors: [] };
  for (const result of results) {
    if (result.status === "fulfilled") summary.fulfilled++;
    else {
      summary.rejected++;
      summary.errors.push(result.reason.message);
    }
  }
  return summary;
};

summarize([
  Promise.resolve("ok"),
  Promise.reject(new Error("bad")),
]).then(console.log);
// { fulfilled: 1, rejected: 1, errors: [ 'bad' ] }

allSettled is the key: Promise.all would reject on the first failure and lose the successful results. Each settled item is discriminated by status, and only rejected items have a reason. The function is async so it always returns a promise, and callers still need a catch for unexpected errors such as a non-iterable argument.

questions.question:
What is printed, and why is the order surprising?

Promise.resolve(1)
  .then((n) => { n + 1; })
  .then((n) => console.log("got:", n));

[1, 2, 3].forEach(async (n) => {
  await Promise.resolve();
  console.log("item", n);
});
console.log("after forEach");

Predict the order and values.

const wait = (ms, v) => new Promise((r) => setTimeout(() => r(v), ms));

Promise.all([wait(20, "a"), Promise.reject(new Error("bad"))])
  .catch((e) => console.log("all:", e.message));

Promise.allSettled([wait(5, "a"), Promise.reject(new Error("bad"))])
  .then((r) => console.log("settled:", r.map((x) => x.status)));

Promise.race([wait(20, "slow"), Promise.reject(new Error("fast"))])
  .catch((e) => console.log("race:", e.message));

Promise.any([Promise.reject(new Error("x")), wait(5, "ok")])
  .then((v) => console.log("any:", v));

