questions.answer:
const withTimeout = (promise, ms) => {
  let timer;
  const timeout = new Promise((_, reject) => {
    timer = setTimeout(() => reject(new Error("Timeout")), ms);
  });
  return Promise.race([promise, timeout]).finally(() => clearTimeout(timer));
};

const wait = (ms, v) => new Promise((r) => setTimeout(() => r(v), ms));

withTimeout(wait(50, "done"), 10).catch((e) => console.log(e.message));
// Timeout

race gives whichever settles first. finally clears the timer on both paths, so a fast success does not leave a stray rejection scheduled (which would otherwise become an unhandled rejection in some designs). The original promise keeps running — promises cannot be cancelled — so the underlying request should also use an AbortController when cancellation matters. Rejecting with an Error, not a string, keeps the catch handler consistent.

